Hi Arianna,

Apologies for the delay in response. If you're fitting a spatial occupancy model for that species and exploring a variety of different models with different covariates, my guess is that the low Bayesian p-value is a result of additional variation in detection probability in the species that you are not accounting for, either spatially and/or temporally within replicates. If the species is extremely rare, then it may be difficult to get a good fit on the species as indicated by the Bayesian p-value. I would encourage you to explore the output from the Bayesian p-value more in depth as opposed to only looking at the summary value, which can help indicate where any lack of fit may be arising from. For example, see some of the graphs made in the introductory vignette. <https://doserlab.com/files/spoccupancy-web/articles/modelfitting#posterior-predictive-checks> If the problem is extra variation in detection probability that cannot be explained by the covariates in your model, then you may explore the use of random effects in your detection model formula. For example, site-level random effects in detection could help account for extra spatial variation in detection probability you aren't accounting for, but these can also be difficult to estimate for a species without a lot of detections.

Kind regards,

Jeff

